/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The first number in 6×19 is the number of strands; the second is the number of wires in each strand.
   - Ropes such as 6×61 go further for very severe bending.
   - In a regular lay, also called cross lay, wires in a strand run opposite to the direction of the strands around the rope.
   - Right-hand and left-hand describe the helix of the strands.
   - For a single-fall lift, choose regular lay.
   - A hoist rope carries more than the payload.
   - Ft is the largest steady pull in the rope in N; g = 9.81 m/s².
   - σb = Er dw / D, where σb is the bending stress in the outer wires in N/mm²; Er is the modulus of the rope, about 0.8 × 10⁵ N/mm² for steel ropes, lower than the 2.1 × 10⁵ N/mm² of the wire because the wires lie in helices; dw is the outer w
   - n = (Fu − Fb) / Ft, where n is the factor of safety with bending counted; Fu is the breaking load of the rope (from the maker’s table, or σuA with σu the ultimate stress on the metallic area).
   - In a block with nf supporting falls and overall efficiency η, the drum-side rope pull is W/(nfη), so more falls allow a thinner rope.
*/
