/* book exam traps: Stopping at the static factor of safety. In the worked example 67 760/9810 = 6.91 looks safe, but counting the rope weight, the starting acceleration and the bending load brings it to 5.67. | Reading 6×19 as 6 wires and 19 strands. It means 6 strands, with 19 wires in each strand. | Using the modulus of steel wire, 2.1 × 10⁵ N/mm², in σb = Erdw/D. The rope modulus, about 0.8 × 10⁵ N/mm², is the one used; the wire value would give 308 N/mm² instead of 117.3 N/mm² in the worked example. | Treating lang lay as the default for a free load. Lang lay is more flexible and wears longer, but it tends to untwist; regular lay is the stable default. | Mixing area definitions. A = 0.4d² for 6×19 is not the same as A = k(π/4)d²; in that second form k is about 0.51 for the same rope. */
